"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 27. The surgical findings indicate a Gleason 4+3 with ISUP grade group 3, pT3a, and positive surgical margins, which are concerning features. Conversely, the seminal vesicles were not invaded, and there was no lymph node metastasis documented. The biopsy showed a Gleason 3+4, which differs from the prostatectomy specimen. The nodal status was documented as no lymph node metastasis.\n\nThe uncertainty in the prognosis is widened by the absence of previous notes, which prevents assessment of postoperative PSA and its trajectory. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and subsequent changes would significantly revise this outlook. The current heuristic horizon suggests a time frame for recurrence. The fixed estimated horizon is 32.85 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 24.63–41.06 months; its coverage has not been validated."
